 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time and Memory Complexity:**
-- The computational complexity of the GPU reconstruction is $O(M)$, growing linearly with the rank $M$, instead of $O(M^3)$ [S1][S2].
-- Memory usage is reduced by the rank reduction and quantization, storing only the Tucker factors and core tensor per brick [S1][S2].
-
-**Measured Performance:**
-- Not given in the sources.
-
-**Scaling:**
-- The method scales with the number of bricks and the rank $M$ [S1][S2].
-
-**Strengths and Limitations:**
-- **Strengths:** Captures non-axis aligned features at different scales better than wavelet transforms using the same amount of coefficients [S1][S2]. Learned bases are data-specific, enabling effective feature extraction and compression [S1][S2].
-- **Limitations:** Pre-processing (decomposition and quantization) can be costly [S1][S2]. The method requires storing Tucker factors for each brick, which may increase memory overhead compared to simpler methods if the rank is not sufficiently reduced [S1][S2].
-
-**When to Use:**
-- Use when the volume data contains non-axis aligned features and efficient GPU-based rendering is required [S1][S2].
-- Use when data-specific basis learning is beneficial for compression [S1][S2].
-
-**When Not to Use:**
-- Avoid when coarse-resolution statistical distribution accuracy is prioritized over non-axis aligned features [S1][S2].
-- Avoid if pre-processing time is a critical constraint and the data does not benefit from learned bases [S1][S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** 
+    *   Progressive Reconstruction has cubic rank complexity $O(R_1 \cdot R_2 \cdot R_3)$ [S1]. For $R=16$, this results in 4096 operations per voxel.
+    *   TTM/n-mode Product Reconstruction has linear rank complexity $O(R)$ (or $O(M)$ where $M$ is the rank) [S1][S2][S3]. This is a 256-fold improvement over progressive reconstruction for $R=16$ [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the storage of the core tensor ($R_1 R_2 R_3$) and factor matrices ($I_1 R_1 + I_2 R_2 + I_3 R_3$). Quantization schemes (8-bit for core, 16-bit for bases) reduce memory footprint [S2][S3].
+*   **Performance:** Optimized GPU implementations assign one kernel thread per voxel, enabling fast random access and efficient decompression before rendering [S2][S3].
+*   **Strengths:** Captures non-axis aligned features at different scales better than wavelet transforms using the same amount of coefficients [S2][S3]. Provides a highly compact model [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Wavelets may outperform TA in reconstructing overall statistical distributions at coarse resolutions [S2][S3]. Pre-processing (decomposition) can be costly [S3].
+*   **Usage:** Applied to interactive large volume visualization using local TA for every brick in a multiresolution hierarchy [S2][S3].
